so this is our second session and in the  first session we spend a bit of time  orienting ourselves in the subject  matter as to why we're studying what  we're studying in this class and how we  will approach our subject matter and  then second part of the our session we  spend together figuring out the issue  that has for as long as I've taught this  course from the start informed or shaped  or puzzled or haunted the discussions in  the class namely the relationship  between religious exclusivism and the  kind of social plurality or possibly  pluralism as a political project that  seems required by globalization  processes we've tried to shed some light  in this and I hopefully it will continue  that discussion as the semester goes on  now today is a designated session for  religion the next session will be for  globalization the two of them are  studied here together so that that's  roughly the plan for the next two  sessions and the heading for our session  today is that of secularization  I think secularization as well as kind  of the obverse of secularization namely  resurgence of religion so what we are  interesting in particular is to look at  what's happening in the context of  globalization within the world of  religions or within the world of faiths  and obviously one topic to discuss and  to look at is the question of possible  secularization partly because there has  been a long-standing tendency to  associate modernity with secularization  and especially folks who think that  globalization is simply extension of  modernity into the onto the whole of the  globe often then associate globalization  processes themselves with secularization  and so this would be our question and to  try to figure out what is happening in  the context of globalization  with the world of of faiths and it's not  just a question of resurgence versus  secularization or demise but it's also a  question of transformation of religions  possibly within the context of  globalization I think what will keep our  eye on maybe not in this session but as  we go along is also what have been  responses of various religions and  religious communities to globalization  processes and you can have those  responses kind of catalogued and mapped  all the way from whole-scale rejection  of globalization as profoundly anti  religious or subverting some of the most  cherished collections of religious  communities and you have these kinds of  responses in different communities  certainly in some Christian communities  as well as in Muslim communities and in  others as well to all the way to the  idea especially within the Christian  tradition that somehow globalization is  a product off is the fruit of a very  fine fruit of some would say of  Christianity itself and then of course  much of it falls in between these these  two kind of differentiated acceptance or  rejections of various elements of  globalization processes we won't have so  much time during this session to discuss  this although maybe in discussion if  some of you are interested in that we  can raise those those questions also a  topic that's gonna be of some interest  to us for this session was we have  assigned to your readings that at least  two of those have a discussion of what  was described as multiple modernity's  basically idea that there's not simple  single way to modernize and especially  in relationship to religion but also  that there are multiple ways in which  modernity has been lived out depending  on cultural circumstances and those  cultural circumstances have often been  shaped by religions now something  analogous can be also said about  globalization itself that there may not  be a single way to globalize but that  the globalization itself as a single  process refracts itself expresses itself  in various localities in a very much  different ways  partly depending on the local culture  and that those local cultures are often  shaped by religious communities that's  going to be also another subject another  way in which we might access the  relationship between faith and  globalization and that would be the  question of how do fades in fact then  influence or shape provide conditions  for globalization processes as you see  from the comments that I've made so far  it's both asking well how does  globalization impact the world of  religion both in terms of its resurgence  possible or demise as well as  transformation and how the world of  religions might impact globalization  processes now that's just to orient us  in this subject matter I think for the  content of the material I wouldn't  presume to speak here with Jose present  so I want to invite Jose Casanova again  one of the one of the not just great  sociologists of religion but great  conversationalist and great public  speakers so it's wonderful to have Jose  again here with us please
 

